VALUES ('7d059e9a-f045-47d6-87c0-db4b365cdf41', 'aws-saa-practice-7', 'AWS SAA-C03 Practice Paper 7', 'aws-saa', ARRAY['CloudTrail', 'IAM Access Analyzer', 'Network Firewall', 'Aurora Backtrack', 'DynamoDB PITR', 'Lambda@Edge', 'Enhanced Networking', 'Cost Anomaly Detection']::TEXT[], 20, 'standard', true, 7, 30);

-- ── Domain: Secure Architectures (30% — 6 questions) ──

VALUES ('dfd4d77e-a5ea-48d2-a4c5-2cbcac014636', '7d059e9a-f045-47d6-87c0-db4b365cdf41', 1, 'A security team needs to answer "which specific IAM principal made this exact API call, from what source IP, and at what time" for a suspicious change discovered on a production resource — not what network traffic occurred, and not whether a resource''s configuration currently matches a compliance baseline. What AWS service is specifically built to answer this kind of "who did what, and when" question?');
VALUES ('dfd4d77e-a5ea-48d2-a4c5-2cbcac014636', '71daeaed-c564-496d-b033-a01030d22665', 'CloudTrail is specifically the API-activity audit log — every management-level (and optionally data-level) API call made in an account gets recorded with the calling identity, the specific action, the timestamp, the source IP, and the request/response details, making it the authoritative source for "who did this, when, and from where" investigations, directly supporting security incident investigation and compliance audit trails. This is a genuinely distinct service from VPC Flow Logs (covered in Paper 2, which records NETWORK-layer traffic metadata — source/destination IP and port, accepted/rejected — with no visibility into WHICH IAM principal or WHAT AWS API action was involved) and from AWS Config (covered in Paper 3, which tracks a resource''s CONFIGURATION STATE over time and compliance drift, not who specifically issued the API call that changed it) — three genuinely different, complementary logging/auditing services answering three different questions, a common and directly testable point of confusion. CloudTrail is a real, foundational, and near-universally-enabled AWS service specifically for this need, not a missing capability.');

VALUES ('175bdd3d-d6f7-4870-82db-11ffb567fa03', '7d059e9a-f045-47d6-87c0-db4b365cdf41', 2, 'A security group allows inbound traffic on port 443 from a specific IP range, but has no explicit outbound rule permitting the RETURN traffic for those same connections. A Network ACL protecting the same subnet has an inbound rule allowing port 443, but no matching outbound rule for ephemeral return ports. Which of these two constructs will still allow the return traffic through automatically, and why?');
VALUES ('175bdd3d-d6f7-4870-82db-11ffb567fa03', 'b2a71802-1ba6-46be-9baa-e1b61a41bdad', 'This stateful-versus-stateless distinction is a foundational, frequently-tested nuance building on the instance-level-versus-subnet-level security group/NACL concept covered generally in Paper 1: a security group tracks the STATE of each connection, so once it permits an inbound request, it automatically recognizes and allows the corresponding response traffic flowing back out, with no separate outbound rule needed for that specific return traffic — this is why security groups are commonly configured with permissive or default outbound rules, since the INBOUND rule is usually doing the real access-control work for a request/response pattern. A NACL, by contrast, evaluates inbound and outbound traffic completely independently against its own separately configured rule sets, with no memory of "this outbound packet is a response to that inbound request" — meaning a NACL needs an explicit outbound rule permitting the ephemeral port range typically used for return traffic, or legitimate responses get silently blocked even though the original inbound request was allowed. This is a genuinely common, real operational gotcha (a security group correctly configured but a NACL missing the ephemeral-port outbound rule) and a frequently tested exam distinction specifically because the two constructs behave so differently despite superficially similar-sounding rule structures.');

VALUES ('49ed2656-c985-4992-ba51-27d09ed9a57c', '7d059e9a-f045-47d6-87c0-db4b365cdf41', 3, 'A company wants to guarantee that objects uploaded to a specific S3 bucket are ALWAYS encrypted in transit — rejecting any request made over plain, unencrypted HTTP rather than HTTPS — enforced at the bucket level regardless of what any individual client happens to use. What bucket policy mechanism enforces this?');
VALUES ('49ed2656-c985-4992-ba51-27d09ed9a57c', '01144e51-ed3a-4246-9909-e669ef6a5523', 'The `aws:SecureTransport` condition key is specifically designed for exactly this enforcement need — a bucket policy statement that explicitly Denies any request where `aws:SecureTransport` evaluates to `false` (meaning the request arrived over plain HTTP rather than HTTPS) makes the encryption-in-transit requirement a SERVER-SIDE guarantee enforced by S3 itself, rather than an assumption resting entirely on every client being correctly configured to use HTTPS — directly connecting to the explicit-deny-always-wins policy evaluation principle covered in Paper 6, since this Deny applies regardless of what any identity-based policy might otherwise allow. This is meaningfully different from SSE-KMS or any at-rest encryption setting (covered generally in Paper 1) — encryption AT REST protects data sitting in S3''s storage, while `aws:SecureTransport` protects data specifically WHILE IN TRANSIT to/from the bucket, directly connecting to the data-states framework (at rest / in transit / in use) covered generally in Security+ content — enabling one does not automatically enforce the other, a genuinely important, testable distinction. S3 Object Lock (covered in Paper 5) is a WORM/retention compliance feature, entirely unrelated to transport encryption enforcement.');

VALUES ('7b07d85e-1b7e-4871-8b5a-887871500229', '7d059e9a-f045-47d6-87c0-db4b365cdf41', 4, 'A company has grown organically for years with dozens of IAM roles and resource policies created by different teams, and worries that some S3 buckets or IAM roles may have unintentionally been left accessible from OUTSIDE the organization''s own AWS accounts, without anyone realizing it. What AWS service is purpose-built to automatically identify this kind of unintended external access across an account or organization?');
VALUES ('7b07d85e-1b7e-4871-8b5a-887871500229', '4ff676a6-5832-4083-b80c-add6f1490a12', 'IAM Access Analyzer is specifically purpose-built for exactly this "did policy sprawl accidentally create external exposure" problem — using logic-based reasoning across an account''s or organization''s resource-based policies and IAM role trust policies, it identifies specific findings where a resource is reachable from outside the defined "zone of trust" (an account, or the whole organization), which is a genuinely hard thing to catch by manually eyeballing dozens or hundreds of individually-reasonable-looking policy statements one at a time, especially as they accumulate gradually over years across different teams. This is a meaningfully different service from GuardDuty (covered in Paper 4, which detects active, ANOMALOUS BEHAVIOR and threat indicators in account activity/network traffic, not static policy analysis) and from Trusted Advisor (covered in Paper 4, which gives broader cost/performance/security best-practice checks, not this specific deep policy-reachability analysis) — Access Analyzer''s specific value is proactively surfacing unintended access BEFORE it''s exploited, a real, purpose-built, and increasingly standard governance tool, not a manual-only problem.');

VALUES ('ec4c0f0f-9e18-406a-9daf-68a044d9b99a', '7d059e9a-f045-47d6-87c0-db4b365cdf41', 5, 'A company wants a managed, stateful firewall that inspects and filters traffic AT THE VPC level — based on domain names, IP reputation, and custom rules — protecting an entire VPC''s traffic (including outbound traffic to the internet) more comprehensively than security groups or NACLs alone provide. What AWS service is purpose-built for this?');
VALUES ('ec4c0f0f-9e18-406a-9daf-68a044d9b99a', '699551dd-894e-408c-9797-0feea3a37e6f', 'AWS Network Firewall fills a genuinely distinct layer in the defense-in-depth stack from security groups and NACLs: rather than simple IP/port/protocol allow-lists at the instance or subnet level, Network Firewall provides STATEFUL, deep packet inspection with support for domain-name-based filtering (e.g., blocking outbound traffic to a specific malicious or disallowed domain regardless of its changing underlying IP), IP reputation-based rules, and custom rule groups including signature-based detection more comparable to a traditional IDS/IPS (covered generally in Security+ content) but applied at the VPC network layer, and can inspect traffic moving between subnets or leaving the VPC entirely, not just what a single instance''s security group happens to allow. WAF (covered in Paper 2, and its rate-based rules in Paper 5) operates at a different layer entirely — inspecting HTTP(S) web APPLICATION requests specifically (SQL injection patterns, XSS, rate limits on web traffic) — not general VPC network traffic across all protocols, a genuinely important distinction between two often-confused "firewall-sounding" services. A Gateway VPC Endpoint (covered in Papers 1 and 6) provides private routing to S3/DynamoDB, entirely unrelated to traffic inspection — this is a real, standard, purpose-built AWS service, not a missing capability.');

VALUES ('b0e02984-89e5-481b-896e-808b295aa936', '7d059e9a-f045-47d6-87c0-db4b365cdf41', 6, 'A company has three VPCs connected to each other using individual VPC Peering connections (A-to-B, B-to-C, but no direct A-to-C peering), and is surprised to discover that instances in VPC A cannot reach instances in VPC C at all, despite both being peered with VPC B. What VPC Peering limitation explains this, and how does it differ from Transit Gateway?');
VALUES ('b0e02984-89e5-481b-896e-808b295aa936', 'f57e669b-e057-45bf-84e9-3c214a3e630f', 'VPC Peering''s non-transitive nature is a genuinely important, frequently-tested architectural limitation: each peering connection is a direct, point-to-point relationship between exactly two VPCs, and traffic cannot "hop through" an intermediate VPC''s peering relationships to reach a third VPC it isn''t directly peered with — meaning a company connecting N VPCs pairwise via peering alone would need a separate peering connection for every single pair (an increasingly unwieldy full-mesh topology as N grows), which is exactly the scaling problem Transit Gateway (covered generally in Paper 5, in the context of a company having grown from 3 to 30+ VPCs) was built to solve — every VPC instead connects ONCE to the central Transit Gateway hub, which then handles routing between every attached VPC without requiring that pairwise full-mesh of individual connections. This is a genuinely real, well-documented VPC Peering limitation (not a misconfiguration in this scenario, and not a limitation Transit Gateway shares) that directly explains why Transit Gateway exists as a distinct, purpose-built solution rather than simply being "VPC Peering with a different name" — a single VPC absolutely can have multiple separate peering connections to different other VPCs, contrary to the last distractor, it just cannot ROUTE THROUGH one to reach another transitively.');

-- ── Domain: Resilient Architectures (26% — 5 questions) ──

VALUES ('75732e68-3023-4215-957a-b8997f954672', '7d059e9a-f045-47d6-87c0-db4b365cdf41', 7, 'A company has a regulatory requirement to maintain a second, independent copy of specific S3 data WITHIN the same AWS Region as the original (not in a different region), so that a scenario like an accidental deletion or an application bug affecting the original bucket doesn''t also affect the compliance copy. What S3 replication feature is purpose-built for this same-region use case?');
VALUES ('75732e68-3023-4215-957a-b8997f954672', '8888e96b-9ba4-4e64-83dc-fc9a58c541f4', 'Same-Region Replication addresses a genuinely distinct requirement from Cross-Region Replication (CRR) — while CRR (covered generally in Papers 1 and 5, typically for DR/global-latency purposes) specifically moves a copy of data OUT of the original region, SRR keeps the replicated copy within the SAME region, which matters directly for regulatory/data-residency requirements that specifically prohibit data from ever leaving a particular region even for backup/compliance purposes, or simply for maintaining a logically separate copy (different account, different bucket, potentially different storage class or lifecycle policy) protected from an accidental deletion or application bug affecting the original bucket specifically, without any cross-region data movement being a factor at all. Both SRR and CRR share the same underlying S3 replication mechanism (requiring versioning enabled on both buckets) but differ specifically in whether the destination bucket sits in the same or a different region — a genuinely real, purpose-built, and directly testable distinction, not a redundant restatement of CRR or an unrelated feature like Object Lock (covered in Paper 5, a WORM/retention feature) or Transfer Acceleration (covered in Paper 6, a transfer-speed feature).');

VALUES ('882745fe-cbd7-4697-b057-530c2226f6ce', '7d059e9a-f045-47d6-87c0-db4b365cdf41', 8, 'A company running a DynamoDB table wants the ability to restore that table to any specific second within the last 35 days, protecting against an accidental bulk delete or a bad application deployment that corrupted data — without needing to manually schedule or manage individual backup jobs. What DynamoDB feature provides this?');
VALUES ('882745fe-cbd7-4697-b057-530c2226f6ce', '7e41d8a4-28cf-4ef9-a857-b13532383917', 'DynamoDB PITR mirrors the same underlying "continuous, automatic, restore-to-any-second" concept as RDS PITR (covered in Paper 2), but as DynamoDB''s own equivalent feature for its NoSQL tables — once enabled, DynamoDB continuously captures the changes needed to restore to any specific second within the retention window (up to 35 days), entirely removing the need to manually schedule or manage discrete backup jobs the way traditional periodic backups require, and directly protecting against exactly the kind of accidental bulk-delete or bad-deployment-corruption scenario described. This is a genuinely distinct feature from DynamoDB Global Tables (covered in Paper 3, which provides MULTI-REGION active-active replication for low-latency global access and regional resilience, not point-in-time historical restoration) and from DAX (covered in Paper 2, a caching layer for read performance, entirely unrelated to backup/restore) — three separate DynamoDB features solving three separate problems, a common point of exam confusion given they''re all DynamoDB-specific capabilities. DynamoDB genuinely does support this kind of granular point-in-time restoration, not just full manual backups.');

VALUES ('867c0349-5df5-4c8f-b15b-2a893e163b64', '7d059e9a-f045-47d6-87c0-db4b365cdf41', 9, 'A company running Aurora needs to quickly undo the effects of an accidental, erroneous bulk UPDATE statement that ran a few minutes ago, restoring the database to its state immediately before that statement — without going through a full point-in-time restore to an entirely new database instance, which would take considerably longer for a database of this size. What Aurora-specific feature is purpose-built for this fast, in-place "rewind" capability?');
VALUES ('867c0349-5df5-4c8f-b15b-2a893e163b64', '1a389030-719c-481b-9fb1-025e8a58541c', 'Aurora Backtrack is specifically Aurora''s own distinct capability for this "fast, in-place rewind" need — rather than the standard point-in-time restore pattern (creating an entirely NEW database instance from a backup and a specific recovery point, then redirecting the application to it, which takes real time proportional to the database''s size), Backtrack rewinds the EXISTING cluster in place to an earlier point within a configured backtrack window, making it meaningfully faster specifically for undoing a recent, well-understood mistake like an erroneous bulk update, where the desired recovery point is close to the current time. This is a genuinely distinct feature from Aurora Global Database (covered in Paper 6, addressing cross-region replication and read latency, not point-in-time rewind) and from Aurora Serverless v2 (covered in Paper 5, addressing auto-scaling capacity, not backup/recovery) — three separate, purpose-built Aurora capabilities solving three separate problems, each a real, standard, and directly testable feature rather than Aurora lacking any fast-recovery option beyond a full restore.');

VALUES ('471bef5d-7e7b-4ce7-8a3f-03042c2717f1', '7d059e9a-f045-47d6-87c0-db4b365cdf41', 10, 'A company has S3 Versioning enabled on a sensitive bucket, but is concerned that even with versioning on, a compromised or malicious IAM credential could still PERMANENTLY delete specific object versions outright, or even disable versioning itself, undermining the protection versioning is meant to provide. What additional S3 setting specifically requires MFA authentication for these particularly destructive actions?');
VALUES ('471bef5d-7e7b-4ce7-8a3f-03042c2717f1', '394ca793-845c-4424-a911-042ddd1a328f', 'MFA Delete adds a genuinely meaningful additional layer specifically closing a real gap in standard S3 Versioning (covered generally in Paper 1) on its own: without MFA Delete, versioning protects against accidental overwrites/deletes (since a delete just adds a delete marker, and prior versions remain recoverable), but a sufficiently privileged credential can still issue a PERMANENT version-delete request or suspend versioning entirely, removing that protection going forward — exactly the scenario a compromised credential (whether stolen or from a malicious insider, covered in Security+ content) could exploit. Requiring MFA specifically for those two particularly destructive actions (permanent version deletion, and changing versioning state) means a stolen access key/secret alone is insufficient — the attacker would ALSO need the registered MFA device, directly connecting to the broader MFA concept covered generally elsewhere as an extra-factor defense. This is meaningfully different from S3 Object Lock (covered in Paper 5, a WORM-style retention/legal-hold feature enforcing IMMUTABILITY for a defined period regardless of credentials, a stronger but differently-mechanized protection) — MFA Delete specifically adds an authentication BARRIER to the destructive action, while Object Lock makes the action IMPOSSIBLE outright during the lock period; genuinely complementary, not identical, features.');

VALUES ('f4dbb689-dcb7-4199-a598-f2c0bd0f1de0', '7d059e9a-f045-47d6-87c0-db4b365cdf41', 11, 'A company mounts a shared file system from multiple EC2 instances spread across three different Availability Zones, and needs that file system to remain fully available even if one entire Availability Zone becomes unreachable, with no manual failover step required. Without any additional configuration beyond a standard setup, does EFS provide this Multi-AZ resilience, and how does this compare to a standard EBS volume?');
VALUES ('f4dbb689-dcb7-4199-a598-f2c0bd0f1de0', '9476e5e4-89ac-456f-bfd8-9838c844fc9e', 'EFS''s Multi-AZ resilience is a fundamental, built-in architectural characteristic rather than an opt-in feature — as a REGIONAL file system (covered generally in Paper 2 for its multi-instance mountability, refined here specifically on its availability characteristic), EFS automatically stores file data redundantly across multiple Availability Zones within the chosen Region by default, meaning instances in any of those AZs can mount and access the same file system, and the loss of any single AZ does not make the file system as a whole unavailable — genuinely no additional Multi-AZ configuration step is required to get this characteristic, it''s inherent to the service. This stands in real, meaningful contrast to a standard EBS volume, which is inherently and permanently tied to a SINGLE specific Availability Zone at creation — an instance in a different AZ cannot directly attach to that same EBS volume at all, and achieving any cross-AZ EBS resilience requires a separate mechanism entirely (like a snapshot copied and restored to a volume in a different AZ, a different pattern from EFS''s native Multi-AZ design). This is a genuinely important, directly testable architectural distinction between the two storage services, not an equivalence between them.');

-- ── Domain: High-Performing Architectures (24% — 5 questions) ──

VALUES ('42b365e5-bccd-432d-9d2c-f1925d8a187f', '7d059e9a-f045-47d6-87c0-db4b365cdf41', 12, 'A company is choosing between ElastiCache for Redis and ElastiCache for Memcached for a new caching layer. They specifically need built-in data persistence and replication for high availability, plus support for complex data structures beyond simple key-value pairs (like sorted sets). Which engine fits this need, and what does the other engine offer instead as its main advantage?');
VALUES ('42b365e5-bccd-432d-9d2c-f1925d8a187f', '810b1f41-0e75-4381-b5d7-536d4a875c1a', 'This refines the general ElastiCache concept (covered in Paper 1) with the genuinely important, directly testable engine-choice distinction: Redis is the meaningfully more feature-rich engine — supporting persistence (so cached data can survive a restart rather than being purely ephemeral), built-in replication with automatic failover (a real high-availability characteristic, echoing the Multi-AZ automatic-failover pattern covered generally for RDS in Paper 6, applied here to the caching layer), and richer data structures beyond simple key-value pairs (sorted sets, useful for leaderboards; lists; hashes; pub/sub messaging) — which is exactly why Redis fits requirements needing HA and anything beyond the simplest caching pattern. Memcached, by design, deliberately trades away those richer features for simplicity and a multi-threaded architecture that can achieve higher raw throughput specifically for simple, high-volume key-value lookups with no persistence or replication requirement — a genuinely valid, different choice for a genuinely different, simpler workload profile, not an inferior option in every case. This is a real, standard, and frequently tested engine-selection decision, not a case of the two engines being interchangeable or ElastiCache lacking persistence/replication entirely.');

VALUES ('3df4885a-e92a-497b-b96f-f7709b154266', '7d059e9a-f045-47d6-87c0-db4b365cdf41', 13, 'A company runs a network-intensive workload on EC2 (a high-throughput data processing application) and wants to minimize network latency, reduce jitter, and increase packets-per-second capacity beyond what the default virtualized network interface provides, without changing instance type. What EC2 networking feature specifically improves these characteristics?');
VALUES ('3df4885a-e92a-497b-b96f-f7709b154266', 'fc8bfd38-6247-4dce-9786-bf68e59a67b6', 'Enhanced Networking specifically targets raw network performance characteristics — packets-per-second capacity, latency, and jitter — using SR-IOV to give the instance more direct, less-virtualization-overhead access to the underlying network hardware, distinct from EBS optimization (covered in Paper 6, which specifically provides dedicated bandwidth for STORAGE/EBS traffic, not general network packet performance) and from Placement Groups (covered in Paper 3 in a cluster/HPC context, which affects instance PLACEMENT relative to each other for lower inter-instance latency via physical proximity, a related but genuinely distinct mechanism from the network-interface-level improvement Enhanced Networking provides — the two are frequently used TOGETHER for the most latency-sensitive workloads, complementary rather than identical). Most current-generation instance types provide Enhanced Networking by default, meaningfully improving network performance without necessarily requiring an instance type change at all — this is a real, standard, and directly relevant feature for network-intensive workloads, not a fixed, unchangeable ceiling.');

VALUES ('4c87b3f8-c788-43fc-862c-c49f3f489cdb', '7d059e9a-f045-47d6-87c0-db4b365cdf41', 14, 'A media application needs to let users stream and seek within very large video files stored in S3 (many gigabytes each) without downloading the entire file first — jumping directly to a specific timestamp should only require fetching the specific portion of the file needed for that segment. What S3 capability supports retrieving only a specific portion of a large object rather than the whole thing?');
VALUES ('4c87b3f8-c788-43fc-862c-c49f3f489cdb', 'c1996282-d668-446d-8aa5-59010fecb8b5', 'Byte-range GET requests directly address this exact "seek within a large file without downloading it all" need — by including a standard HTTP `Range` header on a GET request, a client requests only specific bytes of an object rather than its entirety, which is exactly the mechanism video streaming/seeking relies on (jumping to a timestamp translates to requesting the byte range corresponding to that portion of the encoded file) and is also useful more generally for downloading a single very large object faster by fetching multiple non-overlapping ranges IN PARALLEL rather than one single sequential download. This is a genuinely different mechanism and use case from S3 Multipart Upload (covered in Paper 3, which is specifically about UPLOADING a large object in parts, not retrieving portions of an existing one) and from S3 Select (covered in Paper 3, which retrieves specific ROWS/COLUMNS from a STRUCTURED file like CSV/JSON/Parquet via a query — not arbitrary byte ranges of an unstructured video file, a genuinely different retrieval mechanism entirely) — S3 GET requests genuinely do support this partial-range retrieval as a standard, real HTTP-level capability, not an all-or-nothing download model.');

VALUES ('315c73ff-3728-4809-a479-5053c3a0b228', '7d059e9a-f045-47d6-87c0-db4b365cdf41', 15, 'A company wants to run lightweight custom logic directly at CloudFront''s edge locations — for example, rewriting a request''s URL path, inspecting/modifying headers, or generating a custom response — as close as possible to the end user, without that logic needing to reach all the way back to an origin server for every single request. What AWS capability supports running code directly at the CloudFront edge?');
VALUES ('315c73ff-3728-4809-a479-5053c3a0b228', '472257ec-1353-4e95-8223-382bd489d939', 'Lambda@Edge is specifically designed to run Lambda function code directly within CloudFront''s request/response lifecycle at edge locations around the world, meaningfully different from standard Lambda (covered generally elsewhere, including provisioned concurrency in Paper 6) which runs in a specific AWS Region rather than at globally distributed edge locations — this matters directly for latency-sensitive request-manipulation logic, since executing it at the edge (physically close to the requesting user) avoids the round-trip latency of sending that same logic all the way back to a centralized origin server or Regional Lambda function for every single request, particularly valuable for things like A/B testing logic, authentication token validation, or URL/header manipulation that needs to happen before a request even reaches the origin (or entirely without reaching it, for a fully edge-generated response). Step Functions (covered in Paper 4) orchestrates multi-step workflows, an entirely unrelated capability to edge code execution, and provisioned concurrency (covered in Paper 6) addresses cold-start latency for standard Regional Lambda invocations, not edge placement — this is a real, standard, and purpose-built capability, not a limitation CloudFront lacks.');

VALUES ('5d5d44c1-9b15-4e34-9783-83aa442eff6b', '7d059e9a-f045-47d6-87c0-db4b365cdf41', 16, 'A global company has the same S3 dataset that needs to be accessed by application servers in multiple AWS Regions, and wants each region''s traffic automatically routed to the lowest-latency copy of the data, without the application itself needing separate, hardcoded logic to pick which regional bucket endpoint to use. What S3 feature is purpose-built for this kind of simplified, latency-based multi-region data access?');
VALUES ('5d5d44c1-9b15-4e34-9783-83aa442eff6b', '1fac2c18-6f98-4bcd-a7d2-83a10be26a23', 'S3 Multi-Region Access Points specifically solve the "which of my several regional bucket copies should this request go to" problem by providing ONE global endpoint that AWS''s network automatically routes to the lowest-latency healthy replica for the requesting client — removing the need for an application to embed its own custom, hardcoded region-selection logic, and directly complementing S3 Cross-Region Replication (covered generally in Papers 1 and 5, and refined with Same-Region Replication earlier this paper), which is what actually keeps the underlying regional copies in sync in the first place; Multi-Region Access Points sit on top of that replication as the simplified, unified ACCESS layer. This is a genuinely different concern from S3 Object Lock (covered in Paper 5, a retention/compliance feature) and S3 Requester Pays (covered in Paper 3, a billing-attribution feature for public datasets) — this is a real, purpose-built S3 feature specifically for simplifying multi-region access patterns, not a gap requiring custom application-level routing logic to work around.');

-- ── Domain: Cost-Optimized Architectures (20% — 4 questions) ──

VALUES ('ec396760-29d1-404c-9b10-fbbd2b84d6f8', '7d059e9a-f045-47d6-87c0-db4b365cdf41', 17, 'A company must retain certain compliance records for 10 years, accessed maybe once or twice in that entire period (e.g., for a regulatory audit), and wants the absolute lowest possible S3 storage cost for this data, accepting that retrieval may take up to several hours when genuinely needed. Which S3 storage class fits this specific profile, and what is its key trade-off compared to S3 Glacier (Flexible Retrieval)?');
VALUES ('ec396760-29d1-404c-9b10-fbbd2b84d6f8', '12684af8-33e5-4b61-91c7-9a914f29750c', 'S3 Glacier Deep Archive is specifically S3''s lowest-cost storage tier, purpose-built for exactly this profile — long retention periods (years, not weeks/months) with genuinely rare, non-urgent access — directly connecting to the broader S3 lifecycle/storage-class-selection concepts covered generally across Papers 1 and 2 (IA, Glacier) and Paper 6 (Storage Class Analysis, One Zone-IA), extending that spectrum to its lowest-cost, slowest-retrieval extreme. The genuinely important, directly testable trade-off against standard Glacier (Flexible Retrieval) is specifically RETRIEVAL TIME, not availability or durability (both remain very high across all Glacier tiers) — Deep Archive''s standard retrieval tier takes meaningfully longer (roughly half a day) than Glacier Flexible Retrieval''s standard tier (a few hours), a trade worth making specifically when access is rare enough that the extra wait genuinely doesn''t matter, in exchange for the lowest possible ongoing storage cost over a long retention period. S3 Standard (covered generally in Paper 1) and S3 Intelligent-Tiering (also Paper 1, which optimizes for UNPREDICTABLE, changing access patterns, not this predictable "years of rare access" profile) are both meaningfully more expensive for genuinely long-term, rarely-touched archival data than Deep Archive specifically is.');

VALUES ('6f34cd01-73b4-41fb-8858-581760d03bbc', '7d059e9a-f045-47d6-87c0-db4b365cdf41', 18, 'A finance team wants to be automatically alerted when AWS spending deviates from its normal, expected pattern in a statistically unusual way (e.g., a sudden spike in a specific service''s cost that doesn''t match typical usage trends) — rather than only finding out when a manually-configured fixed dollar threshold in AWS Budgets happens to be crossed, or having to notice it themselves while reviewing Cost Explorer. What AWS service is purpose-built for this kind of automatic, pattern-based spend-anomaly alerting?');
VALUES ('6f34cd01-73b4-41fb-8858-581760d03bbc', '0d9b04f5-a496-45f5-a904-731ea8c5409e', 'Cost Anomaly Detection fills a genuinely distinct gap from the fixed-threshold-based tools covered elsewhere — AWS Budgets (covered generally, with its Actions capability in Paper 5) requires a human to decide and configure a specific dollar threshold in advance, meaning a genuine anomaly that stays UNDER that threshold (but is still wildly unusual relative to normal patterns) would go completely unflagged, and Cost Explorer (covered generally, with forecasting in Paper 5) requires someone to actively notice the deviation while manually reviewing it. Cost Anomaly Detection instead uses machine learning to learn what NORMAL spending actually looks like for the specific account/service over time, and proactively alerts when actual spending deviates from that learned baseline in a statistically meaningful way — directly complementary to, not a replacement for, fixed-threshold Budgets alerts, since the two catch genuinely different kinds of cost problems (a known, hard limit being crossed, versus an unexpected pattern shift that might still be under any given fixed limit). AWS Compute Optimizer (covered in Paper 2) gives resource RIGHT-SIZING recommendations, an entirely different, unrelated function from spend-pattern anomaly alerting.');

VALUES ('6f644598-4c9b-4ef3-8682-0779aa44cedc', '7d059e9a-f045-47d6-87c0-db4b365cdf41', 19, 'A company runs a large, flexible, interruption-tolerant Spot workload and wants AWS to prioritize actually FINDING enough available Spot capacity across multiple instance types/AZs to keep the fleet running with minimal interruption, even if that means paying a slightly higher price for some instances — rather than always choosing strictly the cheapest available Spot price, which might come from a pool more likely to be reclaimed soon. What Spot Fleet allocation strategy fits this priority?');
VALUES ('6f644598-4c9b-4ef3-8682-0779aa44cedc', 'f6ff262a-5b33-430a-9af6-fa17f321d152', 'This refines the general Spot Instance concept (covered in Paper 1, for fault-tolerant, flexible, interruptible batch workloads, and Fargate Spot in Paper 5) with a genuinely important, directly testable operational nuance: Spot Fleet''s `capacity-optimized` allocation strategy specifically prioritizes launching from the pools AWS''s own data indicates have the DEEPEST available capacity (and therefore statistically the lowest near-term interruption risk), even if that means a somewhat higher price than the absolute cheapest currently-available pool — a deliberate trade favoring FLEET STABILITY over squeezing out maximum savings, well suited to workloads where minimizing interruption frequency matters more than shaving the last few percent off cost. The `lowest-price` strategy makes the opposite trade — always chasing the cheapest available price across pools, accepting whatever interruption risk that specific pool happens to carry, which can mean MORE frequent interruptions if the cheapest pool also happens to be a shallower, more contested one. This is a real, configurable, and directly relevant choice for anyone actually running Spot Fleets at scale, not a fixed, non-configurable, one-size-fits-all mechanism, and Spot capacity/interruption risk genuinely does vary meaningfully across pools, instance types, and AZs — that variation is exactly what these allocation strategies exist to navigate.');

VALUES ('f68891b7-a680-41ef-97e6-76cbf7782a9d', '7d059e9a-f045-47d6-87c0-db4b365cdf41', 20, 'A company serves a popular set of static assets (images, videos) directly from an S3 bucket to end users worldwide, and is paying substantial S3 data transfer OUT charges as a result, since every single request currently reaches all the way back to the origin bucket. Without changing where the files are stored, what architectural change would meaningfully reduce these S3 data transfer costs?');
VALUES ('f68891b7-a680-41ef-97e6-76cbf7782a9d', 'f09e00c5-7280-474c-b36f-b0704d168032', 'Placing CloudFront (covered generally in Paper 1) in front of an S3 origin directly reduces the volume of requests and data that actually reach the S3 bucket itself — once content is cached at a CloudFront edge location, subsequent requests for that same popular content from nearby users are served directly from the edge cache, never touching the S3 origin at all for those cache-hit requests, meaningfully reducing S3''s own data-transfer-out volume and cost specifically (a distinct benefit from Transfer Acceleration, covered in Paper 6, which speeds up the UPLOAD/transfer path TO S3 rather than reducing repeat-serving costs for popular download content, and from CloudFront origin groups, covered in Paper 6, which addresses origin failover rather than cost). This directly connects to why CloudFront is the standard, recommended pattern for serving popular static content broadly, rather than serving it directly from S3 to every individual requester regardless of how often the same content is requested repeatedly. S3 Requester Pays (covered in Paper 3) shifts data transfer cost to the REQUESTER rather than reducing the bucket owner''s cost for content THEY want to serve for free, and S3 Versioning (covered generally in Paper 1) is an entirely unrelated data-protection feature with no bearing on data transfer cost.');
